<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<sdf version="1.9">
  <!--
    ============================================================================
    FALCON V2 - Gazebo Sim Harmonic model, STRUCTURAL V1
    ============================================================================
    Author (this pass): geometry-structure specialist agent, 2026-08-21.
    Scope: physical/mechanical skeleton ONLY - links, joints, mass, CG,
    inertia, visual mesh placement, collision primitives, hinge/prop joint
    geometry. NO aerodynamic force/moment model, NO propulsion thrust/torque
    model, NO ArduPilot/SITL/MAVLink integration, NO control-actuation logic,
    and NO plugins of any kind are present in this file. Movable joints exist
    but carry no actuator and produce no aerodynamic/propulsive force.

    Coordinate frame: Gazebo/CAD FLU body frame throughout (+X forward,
    +Y left, +Z up), per CLAUDE.md. All 12 STL meshes share one common
    global/assembly coordinate system (docs/source_of_truth/geometry/
    GEOMETRY.md section 13.2) - no mesh was re-origined or re-centered; raw
    mesh coordinates are used with only the STL_SCALE_TO_SI = 0.001 scale
    factor applied (GEOMETRY.md section 13.1, status
    DERIVED_WITH_STRONG_EVIDENCE: strong convergent numeric evidence for
    millimeters, not a CAD-metadata confirmation). No XFLR5-frame value is
    used anywhere in this file.

    Primary source-of-truth documents (all read in full before authoring
    this file; see also the updates made alongside this file):
      - CLAUDE.md
      - docs/source_of_truth/master/FALCON_V2_MASTER_DATASET_BEFORE_GAZEBO.txt
      - docs/source_of_truth/geometry/GEOMETRY.md
      - docs/source_of_truth/mass_properties/MASS_PROPERTIES.md
      - docs/source_of_truth/controls/CONTROLS.md
      - docs/source_of_truth/propulsion/PROPULSION.md

    ============================================================================
    MASS DISTRIBUTION STRATEGY (chosen and documented here; see also
    GEOMETRY.md "Mass Distribution Strategy" section and the final report)
    ============================================================================
    HYBRID of strategy (A) and (B), chosen deliberately over a pure (B):
      - left_prop / right_prop: assigned their REAL, CONFIRMED component
        mass (0.0301 kg each, master dataset section 44): real sourced data
        exists for this component, so an arbitrary near-zero placeholder
        would be *less* accurate for no benefit. NOT tagged
        TEMPORARY_NUMERICAL_MASS (it is real data), but each prop's
        ROTATIONAL INERTIA below IS tagged V1_PROVISIONAL_ROTATIONAL_INERTIA
        (exact prop rotational inertia is not known; see per-link comment).
      - left_aileron / right_aileron / left_elevator / right_elevator /
        rudder: no component-level mass exists anywhere in the source of
        truth for these specific parts (MASS_PROPERTIES.md section 6:
        airframe structural mass is an undifferentiated approx 3.58 kg
        DATA_REQUIRED remainder). Each is assigned a TEMPORARY_NUMERICAL_MASS
        of 0.001 kg (1 g): a near-massless numerical minimum, NOT a physical
        claim.
      - base_link carries the remainder: 6.000 - (2*0.0301 + 5*0.001)
        = 6.000 - 0.0652 = 5.9348 kg, so total mass across all 8 links
        sums to EXACTLY 6.000 kg (verified in the closing report).
      - No known component mass is double-counted: motor mass (0.286 kg
        total) stays inside base_link (motors are fixed visuals, not
        separate links, per task architecture) and is NOT separately
        subtracted a second time; propeller mass (0.0602 kg total) is
        subtracted from base_link exactly once, matching its assignment
        to left_prop/right_prop.

    KNOWN, DOCUMENTED LIMITATION (flagged for validation, not silently
    resolved): base_link's <inertial><inertia> below uses the
    V1_PROVISIONAL whole-aircraft tensor (master dataset section 9/70)
    UNMODIFIED, i.e. exactly as documented, with no parallel-axis
    correction for the small mass now moved onto the 7 movable child
    links. base_link's <inertial><mass> is 5.9348 kg, not the 6.000 kg
    the tensor's own documentation basis assumes. This is a deliberate
    choice, not an oversight: the V1 tensor was already an externally
    supplied XFLR5 input (master dataset section 9: "Use Plane Inertia =
    OFF ... bu degerler Mean alanina dogrudan girildi"), not a CAD-derived
    sum over real components, so attempting a rigorous parallel-axis
    subtraction for 7 assumed point masses would fabricate a precision the
    source data does not have (MASS_PROPERTIES.md explicitly forbids
    "silently improving or re-deriving" the V1 tensor without a real
    component mass distribution). The resulting inconsistency is judged
    small (0.0652 kg / 6.000 kg is about 1.09% of total mass, located
    close to the aircraft's central region for the 5 control surfaces and
    at a modest approx 0.30 m lateral / approx 0.13 m longitudinal offset
    for the props) and is left for validation to weigh, not silently
    corrected here. Mass, CG, and inertia are otherwise UNCHANGED from
    their documented source values: no unauthorized modification.

    ============================================================================
    COLLISION STRATEGY (see also GEOMETRY.md update alongside this file)
    ============================================================================
    No raw high-poly STL mesh is used as collision geometry anywhere in
    this file (per task instruction). Primitives only:
      - Fuselage: 3 boxes along X (nose+wing-root band, mid-fuselage,
        tail band), sized from confirmed GEOMETRY.md section 13/25
        bounding-box and slice data. The mid-fuselage box's Y half-width
        has no direct slice measurement in the source of truth and is
        conservatively set equal to the (confirmed) tail-band Y half-width
        (0.280 m) rather than interpolated/guessed narrower: tagged
        ASSUMPTION, deliberately oversized/conservative, never undersized.
      - Left/right wing: 1 box each, using each wing's own full mesh
        bounding box (GEOMETRY.md section 13/15). This is a coarse,
        deliberately conservative (oversized at the tapered tip) V1
        approximation, not a tapered/multi-segment fit: flagged as a
        simplification, not final.
      - Horizontal/vertical tail (fixed portions): NOT given a separate
        collision primitive. Per GEOMETRY.md section 26.3, the fixed
        horizontal-stabilizer and vertical-fin structure is part of
        body.stl, and the fuselage "tail band" box above already fully
        envelops that region (its Y = +/-0.280 m and Z = [0,0.330480] m
        span exceed both the elevator's 240 mm half-span and the rudder's
        299.75 mm max height). Adding a second primitive there would
        duplicate collision volume.
      - Motors (left/right): 1 small box each, using the motor mesh's own
        bounding box. Not explicitly required by the task's collision list
        (which named fuselage/wing/h-tail/v-tail) but added as a
        reasonable, low-cost completeness extension: carries no mass (SDF
        collision geometry is massless; mass is set only in <inertial>).
      - Movable control surfaces (aileron/elevator/rudder): each gets its
        own thin box collision matching its own mesh bounding box,
        expressed in the link's own local frame (offset from the link's
        hinge-point origin): full derivation in the per-link comments
        below.
      - Propellers: NO collision geometry (deliberate V1 decision). A
        rotating thin disk at high RPM has no current physical use-case in
        this structural-only pass (no ground-contact or self-collision
        scenario involves the prop disk specifically), and adding one
        risks spurious/self-collision artifacts once a propulsion plugin
        exists. This may be revisited once propulsion/ArduPilot
        integration exists.

    self_collide is explicitly set false at the model level: several
    movable-surface meshes intentionally abut/slightly overlap their
    parent structure at the hinge line (e.g. aileron vs. wing cutout,
    GEOMETRY.md section 16): enabling self-collision would produce
    spurious contact forces there with no benefit for this
    structural-only pass.

    ============================================================================
    HINGE-AXIS FITTING METHOD (aileron / elevator / rudder): summary; full
    method, data, and residuals reported in the closing report and in
    docs/source_of_truth/geometry/GEOMETRY.md (hinge-fit update)
    ============================================================================
    Method (CORRECTED, validation review pass - see GEOMETRY.md section
    33.1 for the full before/after record; this replaces an earlier
    version of this comment that used a fixed-radius-tolerance vertex
    average, found by validation not to be reproducible for the secondary
    (tilt) axis component): direct re-parse of the relevant STL file's own
    vertex data (read-only; no mesh file modified), sliced into narrow
    bands (+/-3 mm) centered on each master-dataset-documented %chord
    station (elevator master dataset section 21; rudder section 29;
    aileron section 33-34). Within each band, the forward-most (hinge-side)
    point is found by a DETERMINISTIC, exactly-reproducible rule: take the
    single greatest-X value in the band (xmax), then collect every vertex
    within 0.001 mm of xmax (true float32-duplicate points, arising from
    STL's per-triangle unshared vertex storage: the same physical point is
    stored once per adjacent triangle), round to 3 decimal mm and take the
    UNIQUE spatial points (not multiplicity-weighted - triangle-density
    around a vertex varies with local mesh topology and must not bias the
    average), then average those unique point(s). This eliminates the
    band-width/tolerance sensitivity that caused the secondary-axis
    non-reproducibility: a wider fixed-radius tolerance was pulling in
    unrelated nearby vertices (a different part of the local surface, or,
    for the rudder, an uneven mix of left/right skin-duplicate counts)
    whose distance from xmax happened to fall inside the tolerance, biasing
    the secondary-axis (Y or Z) mean in a way that was not stable under
    reasonable tolerance choices - exactly what validation's independent
    re-derivation surfaced. The station's own independent variable (Y for
    aileron/elevator, Z for rudder) is taken from the SAME extremal
    point(s)' actual coordinate, not the nominal master-dataset target
    (e.g. the nominal y=70 mm station's true extremal vertex sits at
    y=72.616 mm - a few mm off nominal, expected since we are locating a
    real mesh feature within a band, not assuming it sits at the band
    center). A 2D least-squares line was then fit through these per-station
    points (X, and the secondary axis, each regressed linearly against the
    station's own actual coordinate).
    Boundary-station exclusion (elevator, rudder): the two stations sitting
    exactly at the movable surface's own mesh Y/Z boundary (elevator
    y=50.6/240 mm; rudder z=130.5/299.0 mm) were EXCLUDED: both
    independently return X = -472.684 mm, exactly the mesh's own global
    bounding-box max-X vertex, at BOTH the root AND tip band, ~2 mm off the
    smooth trend defined by the interior stations - an end-cap/boundary-
    face contamination artifact (the flat face closing off the mesh at its
    own Y/Z extremity), not the true local hinge chord position. Confirmed
    reproducible under the corrected deterministic method (not an artifact
    of the old tolerance-average method).
    Aileron tip-station exclusion (secondary/Z axis only, new finding this
    pass): station y=784.875 mm's extremal vertex (Y=782.582, Z=128.564 mm)
    is a local outlier ~12 mm above the smooth Z(Y) trend established by
    Y=700-780 mm (which rises smoothly from Z~115.8 to Z~116.9 mm over that
    range) - confirmed via a band-width sensitivity scan to be a single,
    isolated tip-proximity artifact vertex (station y=784.875 sits only
    4.865 mm from the aileron mesh's own tip boundary at y=789.740 mm,
    likely a tip-cap/closing-face vertex). The PRIMARY (X) value at this
    same station (X=33.396 mm) is NOT similarly affected - it stays
    consistent with the smooth X(Y) trend - so the aileron's X-fit still
    safely uses all 4 given stations, while its Z-fit (secondary/tilt
    axis) uses only the 3 non-tip stations (313.950, 470.925, 627.900 mm).
    Rudder's lateral (Y) hinge position: NOT forced by assumption - it now
    has direct, rigorous per-station evidence. At every interior station,
    the true extremal-X point is a MIRROR-SYMMETRIC PAIR of distinct
    vertices (Y = +d and Y = -d, essentially identical X and Z) - the
    hinge cut face's two skin surfaces (left/right of the thin rudder,
    Y-thickness ~8-9 mm) meet at almost exactly the same X. Averaging the
    two UNIQUE points of each pair gives Y = 0.0000 mm exactly, at every
    single interior station (5 of 5) - not "noise consistent with Y=0" (an
    earlier, weaker characterization this comment previously used) but a
    direct, exact, per-station geometric confirmation. A prior version of
    this analysis reported Y-values of a few tenths of a mm using the
    tolerance-average method and separately, validation's own
    re-derivation using a naive single-vertex tie-break reported -3.6 to
    -4.4 mm - both are explained by the same underlying tie: any method
    that does not correctly identify and evenly average the true mirror
    pair will read out an arbitrary, method-dependent value between 0 and
    the pair's own +/-d spread, which is exactly what both those imperfect
    methods did.

    Residuals (RMS / max, mm, PRIMARY axis = X for all three; interior/
    non-outlier stations only, corrected deterministic method):
      - Elevator (4 interior stations: y=70,130,190,220 mm): RMS 0.0096
        mm, max 0.0153 mm.
      - Rudder (5 interior stations: z=145,180,215,250,285 mm): RMS 0.0263
        mm, max 0.0349 mm.
      - Aileron (4 stations: y=313.95,470.93,627.90,784.88 mm): RMS
        0.0123 mm, max 0.0204 mm.
    Residuals (RMS / max, mm, SECONDARY/tilt axis - Z for elevator/aileron,
    Y for rudder - reported separately per validation's request, not
    previously published):
      - Elevator Z(Y), 4 interior stations: RMS 0.0003 mm, max 0.0004 mm.
        Fitted slope dZ/dY = -0.002072 (tilt approx -0.119 deg) - an ORDER
        OF MAGNITUDE smaller than an earlier, incorrect version of this
        file's dZ/dY=-0.022208 (tilt approx -1.27 deg), which was an
        artifact of the old tolerance-average method, not a real feature.
      - Rudder Y(Z), 5 interior stations: RMS 0.0000 mm, max 0.0000 mm
        (exact, by the mirror-pair-averaging construction above). Y is
        therefore set to exactly 0 with direct per-station evidence, not
        assumption.
      - Aileron Z(Y), 3 non-tip-outlier stations: RMS 0.0020 mm, max
        0.0028 mm. Fitted slope dZ/dY = +0.014147 (tilt approx +0.81 deg) -
        different from an earlier, incorrect version of this file's
        dZ/dY=+0.019252 (tilt approx +1.10 deg, computed including the
        tip-outlier station), and also different from validation's own
        rough independent estimate (dZ/dY approx 0.025-0.036, which their
        review noted was itself "depending on band-width/vertex-selection"
        - consistent with that estimate also being affected by the same
        tip-proximity artifact under a less deterministic method).
    All three PRIMARY (X) fits remain sub-0.04 mm RMS, essentially a
    straight line in each case, consistent with GEOMETRY.md's prior
    qualitative "nearly straight, mildly swept" characterization (section
    17/18/27). The SECONDARY (tilt) axis is now also reproducibly fit,
    with residuals reported for the first time, rather than asserted from
    an unstable, tolerance-dependent average.

    Left/right mirror symmetry: aileron and elevator hinge lines are
    derived for the right side by an exact Y-mirror of the left-side fit
    (right Y = -left Y; right dX/dY = -left dX/dY; right dZ/dY = -left
    dZ/dY): consistent with the already-CONFIRMED Y-mirror symmetry of the
    underlying meshes (GEOMETRY.md section 22, deltas <=0.052 mm for every
    L/R pair). No independent right-side STL re-fit was performed since
    the source meshes are themselves confirmed exact mirrors; a full
    independent right-side re-fit is left as an optional cross-check for
    validation.

    SIGN CONVENTION: SIGN_TEST_REQUIRED for all 5 control-surface joints.
    The <axis><xyz> vectors below define the joint's ROTATION AXIS
    direction only: they do NOT assert which physical rotation direction
    (e.g. trailing-edge-up vs. -down) corresponds to a positive commanded
    joint angle in Gazebo. Per CONTROLS.md section 4, this must be
    resolved later by ELEVATOR_TEST / AILERON_TEST / RUDDER_TEST
    (gazebo-testing owns execution; controls-integration owns the actuator
    once tested).

    ============================================================================
    PROPELLER LINKS/JOINTS
    ============================================================================
    Hub positions (master dataset section 46, CONFIRMED, GEOMETRY.md
    section 7):
      left  hub = (0.2951, +0.3000, 0.1271) m
      right hub = (0.2951, -0.3000, 0.1271) m
    Axis: +X for both (master dataset section 48: measured prop-face
    normal (+0.999996, +0.000018, -0.002668), essentially pure +X, approx
    0.153 deg off).
    Joint type: revolute with a very large <limit> (+/-1e16 rad) to
    emulate continuous rotation: SDF has no dedicated "continuous" joint
    type.
    Rotation DIRECTION (left=CCW, right=CW, master dataset section 44) is
    a physical fact recorded here in comments only: it is NOT encoded by
    the joint definition itself (a single +X axis has no inherent "CCW vs
    CW" until a plugin commands a signed velocity/torque; no propulsion
    plugin exists in this pass, so nothing here could enforce it even if
    it were encoded).
    VIEWING-REFERENCE-FRAME CAVEAT (correction, validation review pass):
    master dataset section 44 states only the bare fact "Left prop = CCW /
    Right prop = CW" - it does NOT state a viewing reference frame (e.g.
    "as viewed from behind/pilot's-eye" vs. "as viewed from the front"),
    and no other repository document (PROPULSION.md, CONTROLS.md,
    GEOMETRY.md) supplies one either. CCW/CW is not a physically meaningful
    statement without a stated viewpoint (the same physical rotation looks
    CCW from one end of the shaft and CW from the other), so this specific
    viewing-frame detail is DATA_REQUIRED, not assumed here. A prior
    version of this comment asserted "as viewed from behind" without a
    supporting citation - that was an unsourced claim, not a documented
    fact, and has been removed rather than re-labeled ASSUMPTION, since no
    reasoned basis for picking one specific viewpoint over another exists
    in the source of truth. This must not be lost by a future
    propulsion-plugin author: left propeller commanded velocity must be
    positive-CCW and right must be positive-CW, but WHICH viewing
    convention "CCW"/"CW" was measured against must be confirmed from the
    manufacturer manual or CAD source before any signed velocity command
    is written - SIGN_TEST_REQUIRED and VIEWPOINT_CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED,
    neither resolved here.

    VISUAL-ONLY propeller scale correction: APPLIED, scoped to <visual>
    only (never <collision>, none exists for props, and never consumed by
    any physics in this structural-only pass, since no propulsion plugin
    exists yet). STL mesh propeller diameter approx 0.2734 m is
    VISUAL_MESH_ONLY (GEOMETRY.md section 20-21.2) and must NEVER be used
    by any future physics code; the real physical propeller for ALL
    propulsion/physics calculations is APC 13x6.5E, D = 0.3302 m
    (PROPULSION.md section 0). Decision: apply an additional 1.208x
    visual-only enlargement (0.001 * 1.208 = 0.001208) to the propeller
    <visual><mesh><scale> ONLY, so the model displays at a realistic size.
    Because a naive uniform scale multiplies ALL vertex coordinates (not
    just apparent size) about the mesh's own distant local (0,0,0) origin,
    applying 0.001208 directly would also shift the visually-rendered hub
    approx 60 mm away from its true position. To keep the enlarged disk
    centered on the real hub, the <visual><pose> translation was computed
    as T = -1.208 * hub_position_m (derived and verified numerically to
    reproduce the hub to less than 0.1 mm; see per-link comment). No
    <collision> exists for props, so this scale never has an opportunity
    to leak into any future collision or physics computation.
  -->

  <model name="falcon_v2">
    <static>false</static>
    <self_collide>false</self_collide>
    <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>

    <!-- ============================================================
         BASE LINK: fixed structure - body, both wings, both motor
         nacelles (visual only), full aircraft mass minus the 7 movable
         child links, Gazebo/CAD CG, V1_PROVISIONAL whole-aircraft
         inertia.
         ============================================================ -->
    <link name="base_link">
      <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>

      <inertial>
        <!--
          CG: Gazebo/CAD reference CG (0.168309, 0, 0.100000) m -
          CONFIRMED, MASS_PROPERTIES.md section 3.1/3.4. This is the ONLY
          CG used anywhere in this file; the XFLR5 reference CG (0.0637,
          0, -0.0210) is NEVER used for SDF purposes (CLAUDE.md CG-duality
          rule).
          Mass: 5.9348 kg = 6.000 kg total - 0.0652 kg on the 7 movable
          child links (see mass-distribution-strategy header comment).
          Inertia: V1_PROVISIONAL whole-aircraft tensor, UNMODIFIED, about
          the Gazebo/CAD CG, master dataset section 9/70,
          MASS_PROPERTIES.md section 5.1.
          Ixz sign convention vs. SDF's expected off-diagonal sign is
          EXPLICITLY UNVERIFIED (MASS_PROPERTIES.md section 5.1): entered
          here exactly as documented (+0.01485), flagged, not silently
          resolved. validation must check this before this value is
          treated as final.
        -->
        <pose>0.168309 0 0.100000 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>5.9348</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.7284</ixx>
          <iyy>0.2507</iyy>
          <izz>0.9523</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0.01485</ixz>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>

      <!-- Fixed visuals: mesh in raw global/assembly coordinates,
           strategy (A): link origin = model origin = (0,0,0), only the
           STL_SCALE_TO_SI=0.001 scale applied, no additional visual pose
           offset, since the mesh already carries the correct absolute
           position for every fixed part. -->
      <visual name="body_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/body.stl</uri>
            <scale>0.001 0.001 0.001</scale>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
      </visual>

      <visual name="left_wing_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/left_wing.stl</uri>
            <scale>0.001 0.001 0.001</scale>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
      </visual>

      <visual name="right_wing_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/right_wing.stl</uri>
            <scale>0.001 0.001 0.001</scale>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
      </visual>

      <!--
        Motor visuals: mesh kept at its own raw global coordinates
        (strategy A, no re-offset). Per task instruction, this uses the
        master-dataset/GEOMETRY.md section 7 "motor center" reference -
        (0.2623, +/-0.3000, 0.1269) m - for POSITIONING PURPOSES only
        insofar as that is the value the mesh's own placement is
        cross-checked against; the mesh itself is NOT re-translated to
        that point (it is already authored in shared global coordinates
        matching it to within the documented approx 7.3 mm
        bounding-box-vs-named-center difference noted in GEOMETRY.md
        section 7, which is explicitly NOT resolved by re-offsetting the
        mesh - doing so would be an unauthorized mesh/visual placement
        change beyond this task's scope). The raw mesh, at scale 0.001
        and zero additional pose, is what is rendered.
      -->
      <visual name="left_motor_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/left_motor.stl</uri>
            <scale>0.001 0.001 0.001</scale>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
      </visual>

      <visual name="right_motor_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/right_motor.stl</uri>
            <scale>0.001 0.001 0.001</scale>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
      </visual>

      <!-- Fixed collision: primitives only, see collision-strategy
           header comment for full reasoning and provenance of every
           box. -->

      <!-- Fuselage box 1: nose + wing-root band. X in
           [-0.027512,0.526880] (nose tip 0.526880 m confirmed
           GEOMETRY.md section 13/14; -0.027512 m = wing mesh's own
           forward X bound). Y=+/-0.094649 m = CONFIRMED wing-root-station
           body slice (GEOMETRY.md section 25). Z=[0,0.330480] m = full
           confirmed body Z bound (no nose-specific Z slice exists; using
           the full range is a deliberately conservative/oversized
           choice, not a measured nose cross-section). -->
      <collision name="fuselage_collision_fwd">
        <pose>0.249684 0 0.165240 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.554392 0.189298 0.330480</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- Fuselage box 2: mid-fuselage, between the wing-root band and
           the tail band. X in [-0.472684,-0.027512] m (spans between the
           wing mesh's forward bound and the elevator/rudder mesh's
           aft-most X bound). Y=+/-0.280000 m is ASSUMPTION: no confirmed
           mid-fuselage slice exists in the source of truth; the
           confirmed tail-band half-width (0.280 m, wider than the
           confirmed wing-root half-width of 0.094649 m) is used here as
           a deliberately conservative (oversized, never undersized)
           stand-in. Z full body range as above. -->
      <collision name="fuselage_collision_mid">
        <pose>-0.250098 0 0.165240 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.445172 0.560000 0.330480</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- Fuselage box 3: tail band. X in [-0.526880,-0.472684] m (tail
           tip to the elevator/rudder mesh's own forward X bound).
           Y=+/-0.280000 m = CONFIRMED tail-station body slice
           (GEOMETRY.md section 25): this same box also serves as the
           horizontal/vertical-tail fixed-structure collision volume, per
           the collision-strategy header comment (its Y and Z extents
           exceed both the elevator half-span and the rudder height). Z
           full body range, CONFIRMED (body's own global max Z occurs in
           this X-band, GEOMETRY.md section 14.1). -->
      <collision name="fuselage_collision_tail">
        <pose>-0.499782 0 0.165240 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.054196 0.560000 0.330480</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

